  [atlas] answer B via nim:stepfun-ai/step-5-preview (59.0s)  critic score: 9.0/10
     No. Glass is an amorphous solid: its atoms are disordered like a liquid but locked in place, so it does not measurably flow over centuries. The uneven thickness of old windows comes from historical glassmaking and installation, not flow.
     sources: https://www.scientificamerican.com/article/fact-or-fiction-glass-liquid/, https://www2.le.ac.uk/offices/press/press-releases/2010/january/glass-is-a-solid-not-a-slow-moving-liquid
  [vega] answer A via nim:stepfun-ai/step-5-preview (4.0s)  critic score: 10.0/10
     No. Glass is an amorphous solid, not a slowly flowing liquid. The uneven thickness sometimes seen in very old windowpanes comes from historical manufacturing methods, such as crown glass and cylinder glass processes, not from centuries of downward flow. At room temperature, glass’s viscosity is so high that any flow is imperceptible on human timescales. The idea that cathedral windows have flowed thicker at the botto
     sources: https://www.scientificamerican.com/article/fact-or-fiction-glass-flows/, https://www.corning.com/glass-and-glass-making/glass-faq, https://www.britannica.com/science/glass-physics
  [orion] answer C via nim:minimaxai/minimax-m3.1-flash (12.7s)  critic score: 9.0/10
     No. The common misconception is that old windows are thicker at the bottom because glass slowly flows. In reality, glass is an amorphous solid: at room temperature, its molecules lack enough mobility for macroscopic flow over centuries. Windowmakers historically produced uneven panes, and later manufacturing improved uniformity; weathering, tilt, and settling of the frame can also create apparent thickness difference
     sources: https://www.smithsonianmag.com/science-nature/why-does-old-glass-have-ripples-and-thicknesses/, https://physicsworld.com/a/glass-is-not-a-slowly-flowing-liquid/, https://www.britannica.com/science/glass

