  [atlas] answer A via nim:stepfun-ai/step-5-preview (45.4s)  critic score: 9.0/10
     No. Lightning frequently strikes the same place twice, or many times. Tall, isolated, pointy objects are especially prone to repeated strikes because they provide a preferred path to ground. A well-known example is the Empire State Building in New York City, which is struck dozens of times each year. The saying is a myth; location, height, and geometry matter more than whether lightning has struck there before.
     sources: https://www.weather.gov/safety/lightning-myths
  [vega] answer B via nim:stepfun-ai/step-5-preview (67.6s)  critic score: 9.0/10
     No. The saying is a myth. Lightning frequently strikes the same place more than once, especially tall, isolated, conductive structures. For example, the Empire State Building is struck many times each year. Lightning tends to seek the easiest path to ground, so repeated strikes to prominent objects are expected.
     sources: https://www.weather.gov/safety/lightning, https://www.nssl.noaa.gov/education/svrwx101/lightning/
